{-
   The `Atomic` module the ownership resolver is built against - `lib/Atomic/Atomic.native.yana`
   written small, on `prelude/Core/Core.yana`'s terms.

   Every declaration is the real one: the same name, the same head, the same attribute and a
   signature the real module would accept. Reading the two side by side is the whole of the
   maintenance, and this file ends by deletion when `-resolver ownership` compiles `lib/`.

   What is *not* here is `lib/Atomic`'s prose, which is where the memory model is argued -
   `doc/spec/concurrency.md` and Analysis-Atomics.md §3 are what these signatures mean. What is also
   not here is the module above them: `Lock`, `SpinLock` and the rest are library code written in
   terms of exactly these declarations, and none of them needs a compiler change.

   Native only, and the *file* says so once rather than thirty declarations repeating it. JavaScript
   has no worker or shared-buffer model in this compiler, so an implementation over ordinary objects
   would be a single-threaded facsimile that conceals a portability error.
-}
module Atomic

{-
   The family `Atomic(a)` admits, and the arithmetic subset of it - §3.2.

   Class spelling so that a generic signature can constrain them, and **closed compiler
   classifications** rather than open extension points: the list is the integers, any payload-free
   enumeration (`Bool` among them) and a raw pointer, and it is closed because every admitted
   operation is *always lock-free*. That is a stronger contract than a sometimes-lock-free generic,
   and the only way to keep it is to refuse the widths a target would have to synthesize with a
   library lock.

   No instance of either is ever written and none may be: `isStructuralClass` in
   `compiler/resolve_mir/select.h` is where that is stated, and the compiler answers both from
   `isAtomicValue` and `isAtomicInteger` - §3.2's family, in one place.
-}
@builtin(atomicValue)
pub class AtomicValue(a)

@builtin(atomicInteger)
pub class AtomicInteger(a)

{-
   The four ordering types - §3.3.

   The repetition is deliberate and is the whole design. `store(x, LoadAcquire)` does not typecheck,
   and there is no relaxed fence that exists only to do nothing, so a diagnostic can say "store takes
   `StoreOrder`" without teaching a matrix.

   An ordering argument must be a compile-time constant. An order changes the generated instructions
   and the optimizer's constraints, so it is not useful runtime state - and requiring it to be
   written at the call is what guarantees good code in an unoptimized build. The constructors are
   numbered in declaration order and the compiler reads that number, so their **order here is part of
   the interface**: `expand_atomic.cpp`'s four tables are the same lists.
-}
pub data LoadOrder = LoadRelaxed | LoadAcquire | LoadSequential
pub data StoreOrder = StoreRelaxed | StoreRelease | StoreSequential

pub data UpdateOrder =
  UpdateRelaxed | UpdateAcquire | UpdateRelease | UpdateAcquireRelease | UpdateSequential

pub data FenceOrder =
  FenceAcquire | FenceRelease | FenceAcquireRelease | FenceSequential

{-
   What a compare-exchange answers - §3.4.

   `Exchanged` exactly when the old value equalled `expected` and `desired` was stored; otherwise
   `Unchanged` carrying what was actually there. Neither form mutates its expected argument and
   neither loses the observed value on failure, which is what lets a retry loop feed the answer
   straight back in as the next attempt's expectation.
-}
pub data ExchangeResult(a) = Exchanged | Unchanged(a)

{-
   Construction and consumption - §3.1.

   An atomic is not implicitly zeroed. `atomic(0)` is already explicit, works for pointers and flags
   alike, and avoids a second initialization rule for generic storage.

   `intoValue` needs no atomic instruction at all: owning the value proves there is no outstanding
   borrow and therefore no concurrent accessor. It is the clean teardown path for a containing
   structure, and the way a test inspects a private atomic after every worker has joined.
-}
@builtin(atomicNew) pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) atomic(initial: a) -> Atomic(a)
@builtin(atomicIntoValue) pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) intoValue(->self: Atomic(a)) -> a

{-
   The basic operations - §3.4.

   `self` is an *immutable borrow*, which is the default convention here and is the one controlled
   form of interior mutation the language has: many threads may hold that borrow at once, and the
   only mutation it enables is one of the operations here. It creates no `&Atomic(a)` and no `&a`.
-}
@builtin(atomicLoad) pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) load(self: Atomic(a), order: LoadOrder) -> a
@builtin(atomicStore) pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) store(self: Atomic(a), desired: a, order: StoreOrder) -> {}
@builtin(atomicExchange) pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) exchange(self: Atomic(a), desired: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a

{-
   Compare and exchange - §3.4 and §3.5.

   `compareExchange` is **strong**: it answers `Unchanged` only when the value it read differed from
   `expected`. `compareExchangeWeak` may answer `Unchanged(expected)` spuriously, and exists for
   retry loops on load-linked/store-conditional machines; on x86 the two lower identically and the
   flag only stops a caller from assuming otherwise.

   The failed comparison's order is derived from the requested one by dropping the release half,
   which has nothing to order on a path that performs no write - `failureOrderFor` is that
   projection. An algorithm that specifically needs a weaker failure path writes `Advanced` below.
-}
@builtin(atomicCompareExchange) pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) compareExchange(
  self: Atomic(a), expected: a, desired: a, order: UpdateOrder
) -> ExchangeResult(a)

@builtin(atomicCompareExchangeWeak) pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) compareExchangeWeak(
  self: Atomic(a), expected: a, desired: a, order: UpdateOrder
) -> ExchangeResult(a)

{-
   The explicit two-order forms - §3.5.

   The compiler rejects a failure order stronger than the success order, a failure path that
   synchronized more than the successful one being something no target implements. Putting these
   behind a longer qualified name is not a judgment that they are unsafe; it says only that the extra
   degree of freedom belongs in code whose algorithm and benchmark justify it.
-}
@builtin(atomicComparePair) pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) Advanced.compareExchange(
  self: Atomic(a), expected: a, desired: a, success: UpdateOrder, failure: LoadOrder
) -> ExchangeResult(a)

@builtin(atomicComparePairWeak) pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) Advanced.compareExchangeWeak(
  self: Atomic(a), expected: a, desired: a, success: UpdateOrder, failure: LoadOrder
) -> ExchangeResult(a)

{-
   The fetch operations - §3.4.

   Every one answers the value from *before* the update. The `fetch` prefix says so, matches what x86
   and LLVM naturally return, and keeps the family consistent. Addition and subtraction wrap at the
   stored width.

   There are deliberately no operator overloads. `counter += 1` looking ordinary while being shared,
   atomic and ordered by an invisible default would violate all three of this surface's goals at once.
-}
@builtin(atomicFetchAdd) pub fn (AtomicInteger(a)) fetchAdd(self: Atomic(a), amount: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a
@builtin(atomicFetchSub) pub fn (AtomicInteger(a)) fetchSub(self: Atomic(a), amount: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a
@builtin(atomicFetchAnd) pub fn (AtomicInteger(a)) fetchAnd(self: Atomic(a), mask: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a
@builtin(atomicFetchOr) pub fn (AtomicInteger(a)) fetchOr(self: Atomic(a), mask: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a
@builtin(atomicFetchXor) pub fn (AtomicInteger(a)) fetchXor(self: Atomic(a), mask: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a

{-
   Fences and spinning - §3.6.

   `fence` orders accesses but is attached to no location. It does not by itself synchronize two
   threads: the surrounding atomic operations still have to establish the relation.

   `spinHint` says the loop containing it is polling. It emits x86 `pause`, AArch64 `yield`, or
   nothing where a target has no useful hint. It is neither a compiler fence nor a memory fence and
   establishes no happens-before edge: a program whose correctness changes when every `spinHint` is
   deleted was already wrong.
-}
@builtin(atomicFence) pub fn fence(order: FenceOrder) -> {}
@builtin(atomicSpinHint) pub fn spinHint() -> {}
